            "marks": [],
            "text": "SEO for real estate: common questions from business owners"
          }
        ],
        "markDefs": [],
        "style": "h2"
      }
    ],
    "textAlign": "left"
  },
  {
    "_key": "cf6de7c7afb1",
    "_type": "faqBlock",
    "faq": {
      "_type": "accordionBlock",
      "items": [
        {
          "_key": "fi-1-mr1upzzb",
          "answer": [
            {
              "_key": "b-2-mr1upzzb",
              "_type": "block",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "s-3-mr1upzzb",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "marks": [],
                  "text": "Real estate SEO faces challenges most industries don't: a large, constantly-changing catalog of listing pages, direct competition with property portals (Rightmove, Idealista, Zillow), and buyers who often research in multiple languages. Effective real estate SEO combines technical architecture for the catalog (handling faceted search and pagination without fragmenting ranking signals), content depth per property type and location, and trust signals — all while keeping the site fast on image-heavy pages."
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "style": "normal"
            }
          ],
          "question": "What makes SEO for real estate different from general SEO?"
        },
        {
          "_key": "fi-4-mr1upzzb",
          "answer": [
            {
              "_key": "b-5-mr1upzzb",
              "_type": "block",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "s-6-mr1upzzb",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "marks": [],
                  "text": "Both. Portals bring volume but give the agency no direct relationship with the buyer — the portal owns the lead. Your own website is the asset you control: the brand, the inquiry flow, and the client data. SEO on your own site builds an audience that comes directly to you, independent of portal algorithm changes or fee increases. Strong real estate businesses rank on both: portals for volume, own site for depth and direct contact."
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "style": "normal"
            }
          ],
          "question": "Should a real estate website compete with property portals, or complement them?"
        },
        {
          "_key": "fi-7-mr1upzzb",
          "answer": [
            {
              "_key": "b-8-mr1upzzb",
              "_type": "block",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "s-9-mr1upzzb",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "marks": [],
                  "text": "Three content types consistently perform well: area and location guides (neighborhood overviews, investment potential, local infrastructure — things a listing page can't answer), property-type pages structured around how buyers search, and buyer guides (how to purchase, financing, legal process). These are what portal listings can't provide and what Google rewards with stable long-term rankings."
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "style": "normal"
            }
          ],
          "question": "What content drives the most organic traffic to real estate sites?"
        },
        {
          "_key": "fi-a-mr1upzzb",
          "answer": [
            {
              "_key": "b-b-mr1upzzb",
              "_type": "block",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "s-c-mr1upzzb",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "marks": [],
                  "text": "Real estate sites are technically complex. Search filters and URL parameters can generate hundreds of overlapping pages, which fragment ranking signals and confuse crawlers. Getting this right — canonical tags, crawl budget management, structured data for listings, and site speed on image-heavy property pages — is one of the highest-leverage technical investments for a real estate website."
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "style": "normal"
            }
          ],
          "question": "How do technical issues affect a real estate catalog specifically?"
        },
        {
          "_key": "fi-d-mr1upzzb",
          "answer": [
            {
              "_key": "b-e-mr1upzzb",
              "_type": "block",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "s-f-mr1upzzb",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "marks": [],
                  "text": "First measurable improvements typically appear within a few months. Significant organic traffic and lead volume builds gradually, usually over 6–12 months, as content authority accumulates and technical improvements propagate through the index. Timeline depends on starting point, competition level, and implementation pace."
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "style": "normal"
            }
          ],
          "question": "How long does real estate SEO take to produce results?"
        },
        {
          "_key": "fi-g-mr1upzzb",
          "answer": [
            {
              "_key": "b-h-mr1upzzb",
              "_type": "block",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "s-i-mr1upzzb",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "marks": [],
                  "text": "Rankings and traffic are inputs, not outcomes. I track what matters for the business: organic lead volume, lead quality (qualified inquiries vs noise), cost-per-organic-lead compared to paid channels, and the growing share of organic in the total lead mix. The goal is a reliable, growing organic channel that steadily reduces dependence on advertising spend."
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "style": "normal"
            }
          ],
          "question": "How do you measure SEO success for a real estate business?"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
